index 5f9731f8..696059cb 100644
--- a/services/field-mcp/README.md
+++ b/services/field-mcp/README.md
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@ Registered only by `ffmcp-write` and only when `FFMCP_ENABLE_WRITES` is exactly
 | `federation_broker_crossing` | decides a crossing; ledgers a federation row | irreversible |
 | `sealed_ledger_export` | writes an auditor bundle on the ledger host; the tool cannot choose the directory (`out_dir` is not offered) | irreversible |
 
-Controls on every write call, in this order: arguments validated (a `reason` of 1 to 500 characters is mandatory; an empty, blank or oversize one is refused before any network call) -> the per-session rate limit (default 10 per minute across all write tools, `FFMCP_WRITE_RATE_PER_MIN` 1 to 60, never off) -> one `mcp.write.intent` row appended to the sealed ledger (tool, target, reason, operator id, client name, session id; never the request body) -> only then the write -> one `mcp.write.outcome` row (call id, tool, `ok`, `error` with its status, `not_sent` or `unknown`; best effort, never the body). If the intent append fails for any reason the write is refused. A write whose request went out but whose result was not seen (a timeout or a broken reply after the request was sent) returns `<service> outcome unknown; do not retry; check with a read tool` and is recorded as `unknown`: the service may have acted, so a retry could act twice. Only a refused connection (nothing was sent) says `unreachable`. The rows are signed as this server's own caller identity when `FFMCP_CALLER_ID` (its name in the ledger's caller keyring) and `FFMCP_CALLER_KEY_FILE` (the path to its Ed25519 private key, PEM) are both set, through field-core's `CallerSigner` and `signed_append_body`, so a ledger started with `FIELD_LEDGER_REQUIRE_CALLER_SIGNATURE=1` accepts them; the key file's text and path are never in an error or a result. Without them the rows go unsigned and such a ledger refuses them, so every write is refused with it (the safe direction, pinned by a test). Registering the public key in the ledger's keyring is the operator's step; no key is created here.
+Controls on every write call, in this order: arguments validated (a `reason` of 1 to 500 characters is mandatory; an empty, blank, invisible-only (zero-width, bidi-control) or oversize one is refused before any network call, and control and invisible characters are removed from the text stored in the ledger row) -> the per-session rate limit (default 10 per minute across all write tools, `FFMCP_WRITE_RATE_PER_MIN` 1 to 60, never off) -> one `mcp.write.intent` row appended to the sealed ledger (tool, target, reason, operator id, client name, session id; never the request body) -> only then the write -> one `mcp.write.outcome` row (call id, tool, `ok`, `error` with its status, `not_sent` or `unknown`; best effort, never the body). If the intent append fails for any reason the write is refused. A write whose request went out but whose result was not seen (a timeout or a broken reply after the request was sent) returns `<service> outcome unknown; do not retry; check with a read tool` and is recorded as `unknown`: the service may have acted, so a retry could act twice. Only a refused connection (nothing was sent) says `unreachable`. The rows are signed as this server's own caller identity when `FFMCP_CALLER_ID` (its name in the ledger's caller keyring) and `FFMCP_CALLER_KEY_FILE` (the path to its Ed25519 private key, PEM) are both set, through field-core's `CallerSigner` and `signed_append_body`, so a ledger started with `FIELD_LEDGER_REQUIRE_CALLER_SIGNATURE=1` accepts them; the key file's text and path are never in an error or a result. Without them the rows go unsigned and such a ledger refuses them, so every write is refused with it (the safe direction, pinned by a test). Registering the public key in the ledger's keyring is the operator's step; no key is created here.
 
 Credentials: a write call uses `FFMCP_<SERVICE>_WRITE_KEY` and never `FFMCP_<SERVICE>_KEY`; a service with no write key is refused, not served with the read key. The operator pair (`FFMCP_OPERATOR`, `FFMCP_OPERATOR_KEY`) goes only to the two Tier 2 routes whose service verifies it, `agent_registry_create_agent` (`POST /agents`) and `sealed_ledger_export` (`POST /export`), and only when both values are set; the other five write tools, the audit append and every read carry no pair. The `operator` id in an audit row is a label the configuration supplied, not a verified identity. Until per-tier platform keys are in force, the platform has one shared secret, so set both to it; the separation is then enforced here, not by the services.
 
